{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's description MUST state when to use it in terms of the user's task. It MAY name another bundled skill to use instead where an agent could confuse the two. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Site query `requirements.cli.skill_conventions/skills`**\n- Run by name, it matches exactly the five `assets/skills/*/SKILL.md` files: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`.\n- A search for `^name:` and `^description:` found no other bundled skill with front matter. The reference files under the skills therefore have no front matter and are correctly not selected.\n- The query neither misses a bundled skill nor selects anything outside the requirement's scope.\n- Its snippets show what it selects and what it must not. The match snippet is a document with `---` front matter. The no_match snippet is a plain `# Example` document with none. That is adequate for a single `kind: minus_metadata` condition.\n\n**Obligations and tests**\n- Each description MUST state when to use the skill in terms of the user's task. `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_descriptions_say_when_to_use_them]` checks this for all five skills in `SKILLS`.\n  - Each description must be present and exactly once, non-empty, at most 1024 characters, and equal to the value read from the file.\n  - Each description must start with a skill-specific \"Use …\" trigger phrase, for example \"Use when adopting Habeas in an existing codebase\" and \"Use when a change moves code, tests, or requirements\".\n  - These phrases describe the user's task, and the `starts_with` assertions would fail if a description dropped its trigger.\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics]` asserts that `SKILLS` equals the directories under `assets/skills`. The per-skill loop therefore covers every site.\n- The MAY clause about naming another bundled skill imposes nothing to check. The test still verifies that any `habeas-*` name a description mentions is a bundled skill. It also checks the specific \"use X instead\" pointers for the confusable pairs: requirements with refactor, requirements with evidence, evidence with refactor, bootstrap with evidence, and refactor with requirements.\n- I did not assess the `.workflow` sub-requirement, which is outside the subject text. Each site is reviewed separately, so I did not vet the sites themselves.\n\nI found no gaps in the query, its snippets, or the tests.",
  "references": "4fbf9a3072386612109271b7cf55673834816c2318c1c11463d91326147378e5",
